            <h2>What is Fasting?</h2>
            <p>
                Fasting is the voluntary abstinence from food and, sometimes, drinks for a specified period. It can range from short periods (like 12-16 hours in intermittent fasting) to longer durations, even extending to several days or weeks. Unlike the typical eating patterns of modern society, fasting limits food intake, which may help mitigate risks associated with overeating and sedentary lifestyles.
            </p>

            <h2>Why Do People Fast?</h2>
            

            <h2>Types of Fasting</h2>
            <p>Fasting can vary in length and frequency:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Intermittent Fasting (IF): This involves shorter, regular fasting periods, such as the 16:8 method (16 hours fasting, 8 hours eating).</li>
                <li>Extended Fasting: Lasting from 24 to 36 hours or even longer, these fasts may be performed less frequently.</li>
                <li>Prolonged Fasting: Fasting for several days to weeks, often for specific health goals or under medical supervision.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Is Fasting Safe?</h2>
            <p>
                Research shows that fasting is generally safe for most people and does not produce significant gastrointestinal issues like nausea or constipation. Intermittent fasting, in particular, has been found to have minimal adverse effects, with potential benefits for body image and mental health.
            </p>

            <h2>Can Fasting Help With Weight Loss?</h2>
            

            <h2>Benefits of Intermittent Fasting</h2>
            <p>Fasting offers numerous potential benefits, including:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Improved Memory and Cognitive Function: Fasting can boost brain health and improve memory.</li>
                <li>Better Heart Health: It may help lower blood pressure and reduce heart disease risk.</li>
                <li>Enhanced Physical Performance: Fasting can aid in fat loss while maintaining muscle mass.</li>
                <li>Improved Insulin Sensitivity: Fasting may help reduce insulin resistance, benefiting those with type 2 diabetes.</li>
                <li>Gut Health: It can improve gut microbiota and reduce inflammation.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>What Can You Consume During Fasting?</h2>
            

            <h2>Can Supplements Be Taken During Fasting?</h2>
            <p>Yes, but it's essential to choose wisely:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Water-Soluble Vitamins: Such as vitamins B and C, can be taken on an empty stomach.</li>
                <li>Electrolytes: Adding natural electrolytes like Himalayan salt can help maintain hydration.</li>
                <li>Avoid: Protein powders and sweetened supplements, as these can break a fast.</li>
            </ul>

